          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.873,
            0.152
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.2.1 Experimental Probability: Performing Observations or Experiments"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.159,
            0.885,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "Another way of obtaining objective estimates for probabilities is to perform an experiment. In an experiment, the event is a result of the experiment and is called an **outcome**. The set of all possible outcomes in an experiment is called the **sample space**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.237,
            0.885,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "The sample space may be listed within brackets, separated by commas as shown below."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.28,
            0.596,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": "**Example 1:** Experiment: A coin is tossed"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.3,
            0.737,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "Possible Outcomes: Heads (H) or Tails (T) (See Fig. 7.2)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.32,
            0.382,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": "Sample Space: {H, T}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.346,
            0.446,
            0.366
          ],
          "content": "Experiment: A die is rolled"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.366,
            0.884,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "Possible Outcomes: The top side of the die shows 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6 dots (See Fig. 7.3)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.405,
            0.471,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "Sample Space: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.43,
            0.485,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.232,
                0.588,
                0.525,
                0.607
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.2: Heads and tails of a coin"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.695,
            0.464,
            0.821,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.662,
                0.588,
                0.857,
                0.607
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.3: A 6-sided die"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.615,
            0.817,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Exprimental} \\mathrm{Probability} = \\frac{\\mathrm{Number of times the event occurred}}{\\mathrm{Total Number of trials}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.668,
            0.886,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "**Example 2:** Suppose you roll a die 50 times, and it lands on a 4 exactly 8 times."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.708,
            0.789,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "Experimental probability of rolling a 4 is $\\frac { 8 } { 5 0 }   =   0 . 1 6$ % 1 or 6 ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.756,
            0.863,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": "We also say that the **relative frequency** of rolling a 4 is $\\frac { 8 } { 5 0 }   =   0 . 1 6$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.796,
            0.886,
            0.875
          ],
          "content": "Relative frequency helps you understand probability based on actual data, rather than theoretical predictions. It is especially useful in statistics and data analysis when you are working with observed outcomes."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
